+# Build menu hitches
+
+## The problem
+
+The menu rebuilt itself all the time:
+
+- Each tab page was built with `switch`, so every tab switch destroyed the old page and built
+  the new one. In the MicroProfiler, Shapes made about 450 instances in one frame (40 ms against
+  an 8 ms baseline) and Colors about 370 (30 ms).
+- `Main.client.luau` mounted the whole panel with `show(isMenuOpen, ...)`, so every open rebuilt
+  the panel and its current page, and every close destroyed them.
+
+## What changed
+
+- **The panel is kept** (`Main.client.luau`). It is built the first time the menu opens and is
+  never destroyed. Closing slides it out the same way as before and then sets it to
+  `Visible = false`, so it is not drawn while closed.
+- **Pages are kept** (`BuildMenu.luau`). `switch` was replaced by `indexes` over a "visited"
+  map: each tab's page is built the first time that tab is shown and is then kept. Pages that
+  aren't selected are hidden with `Visible = false`.
+- **Kept pages reset the way a rebuilt page used to:**
+  - Colors and Shapes scroll back to the top once they go off screen, so they open at the top
+    like before.
+  - The rainbow swatch's hue starts over from red on each visit.
+- **The rainbow swatch's Heartbeat connection runs only while the Colors page is on screen**
+  (the menu is open or still sliding out, and Colors is the selected tab). Before, the swatch was
+  destroyed while you weren't looking at it. Now it is kept, so without this check its connection
+  would keep running when the menu was closed.
+
+The look and the slide animation are the same. One small difference: if you reopen the menu
+while it is still sliding out, the panel now turns around from where it is. Before, a new panel
+slid up from the bottom.
+
+What's still slow: the first time you open the menu, and the first time you visit each tab, the
+page is still built in that frame. Spreading that first build over several frames would change
+when things appear on screen, so I didn't do it.
+
+Memory cost: after you've visited every tab, about 900 extra GUI instances stay alive.
+
+## How to check it in Studio
+
+Before you measure, close every other Studio window and plugin panel you can. Use the same
+window size for the "before" and "after" runs.
+
+1. Start a Play session and open the MicroProfiler (Ctrl+F6).
+2. Open the menu (M) and visit Blocks, Colors, and Shapes once each. These first builds still
+   spike. That's expected.
+3. Pause the profiler (Ctrl+P), or use Ctrl+F6 > Dump, and start from a fresh capture.
+4. Switch between tabs about ten times, then close and open the menu about ten times, with the
+   menu last on Shapes and then last on Colors.
+5. Pause and look at the frames where you clicked:
+   - Frame time should stay near your idle frame (about 8 ms on your laptop). The 30 to 40 ms
+     spikes should be gone.
+   - There should be no big instance creation in those frames. To count it directly, run this in
+     the client's command bar during the test. It should print nothing after the first visits.
+
+     ```lua
+     local gui = game.Players.LocalPlayer.PlayerGui.BuildMenuGui
+     local added = 0
+     gui.DescendantAdded:Connect(function() added += 1 end)
+     game:GetService("RunService").Heartbeat:Connect(function()
+     	if added > 0 then print("instances added this frame:", added) end
+     	added = 0
+     end)
+     ```
+
+6. With the menu closed, open Script Profiler (View > Script Profiler) for a few seconds.
+   `BuildMenu`'s Heartbeat callback (the rainbow swatch) shouldn't appear.
+
+For a before/after comparison, run the same steps on the previous commit
+(`git stash` or check out `28c5a05`) and compare the click frames.
